Saved the fan-perspective review to `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Adela/2026-09-09_121156/fan.md`.

**Verdict:** revise — trust strained, not broken (0 blockers, 3 concerns).

**Strongest hit:** the three-beat childhood inventory (English / Feet / A timetable) — a genuinely new connection between known ballet/language facts and the Type 3 read, executed without leaning on jargon.

**Highest-priority concern (FAN-R1):** the opening line teases "an ill-fitting detour into indie rock" and never returns to it. Live research this pass turned up the real story the shared evidence packet had missed entirely — Adéla told Rolling Stone (Tomás Mier, 2025-10-31, via Yahoo syndication) she came off *Dream Academy* saying "Fuck pop," went emo, made music she now calls "really, really bad," then months later course-corrected back to pop. That's the single most literal, on-the-record illustration of the piece's own identity-recovery thesis, and it's dropped after one clause — along with a second gap where her actual debut EP, *The Provocateur* (Aug 2025), is cited four times as a source URL but never once named as a release.
